\p
\v 14 My¦147147 fellow¦147146 believers¦147146, what would I gain if¦147148 I said¦147150 that someone¦147151 had faith¦147149 but¦147155 it didn't¦147156 alter how they live their life? Faith¦147162 like that wouldn't be able¦147160 to¦147163 save¦147163 them.
\v 15 If a fellow believer is lacking¦147174 clothes or¦147168 food¦147178,
\v 16 and one of you¦147187 says¦147180 to¦147185 them¦147185, ‘Go in¦147190 peace¦147191 and be warm and satisfied¦147195,’ but¦147182 don't¦147197 help with their¦147203 physical needs, what use would that be?
\v 17 In other words, faith¦147211 without the¦147210 corresponding lifestyle is¦147218 just dead¦147217.
\p
\v 18 No doubt someone¦147223 will¦147222 say¦147222, ‘Well you have the faith and I do the good deeds.’ So show¦147245 me your¦147236 faith without¦147237 good deeds and I'll demonstrate my faith \em by\em* my good deeds.
\v 19 You believe that there's one¦147258 \nd God¦147261\nd* and you're¦147264 right, but¦147269 even¦147265 the¦147266 demons¦147267 believe that and shudder¦147270.
\v 20 But the vain¦147276 person¦147275 still wants¦147271 to know¦147273 if faith¦147279 without good deeds is¦147285 wasted?
\v 21 Wasn't our¦147289 ancestor Abraham considered right with God due to his \em actions\em* when he offered¦147294 up¦147294 his son¦147297 Isaac¦147295 on¦147299 the¦147300 altar¦147301?\x + \xo 2:21: \xt Gen 22:1-14.\x*
\v 22 So his faith was working¦147306 together¦147306 with¦147308 his actions, and¦147311 it was by¦147312 his actions that his faith was perfected¦147317.
\v 23 This fulfilled¦147319 the¦147320 scripture¦147321 that¦147322 says¦147323, ‘Abraham¦147326 believed¦147324 in¦147327 God and so he was consider to¦147331 be right¦147333 with God, and was called¦147337 a friend¦147335 of God.’\x + \xo 2:23: a \xt Gen 15:6; \xo b \xt 2Ch 20:7; Isa 41:8.\x*
\v 24 So you can see¦147338 that¦147341 a person¦147345 is¦147344 considered right with God due to his actions, and¦147346 not¦147347 just by¦147348 their ‘faith¦147349’.
\v 25 Similarly the prostitute¦147357 Rahab¦147355 was made right by her actions when she welcomed¦147362 the \add Hebrew\add* messengers¦147365 and¦147366 then¦147353 helped them get away by a different route.\x + \xo 2:25: \xt Josh 2:1-21.\x*
\v 26 Just like a body¦147373 is dead once the spirit¦147375 has departed, so¦147378 too faith¦147381 is dead if it's not demonstrated by good deeds.
\c 3
\s1 The tongue
\rem /s1 Controlling the Tongue; Taming the Tongue; Christian speaking
\p
\v 1 My¦147392 brothers¦147391 and¦147391 sisters¦147391, not everyone should desire to be a teacher¦147389 because we¦147397 know¦147393 that¦147394 us teachers¦147389 will¦147397 be judged¦147396 more strictly.
\v 2 We¦147400 all¦147401 stumble in¦147404 many¦147398 ways¦147398. If there was someone¦147403 who never said anything wrong, this¦147408 person would be perfect¦147409 and also able to have self-control over their entire¦147401 body¦147417.
\v 3 With horses¦147424, we can put¦147432 the bridle¦147426 in it's mouth¦147430 so we can make them do what we want, and¦147421 so we can direct¦147445 the whole¦147441 animal.
\v 4 Similarly, even huge¦147452 ships¦147450 that are being¦147453 driven¦147459 on by strong winds¦147457 are controlled by a small rudder¦147463 and¦147454 can be steered wherever¦147464 the captain desires.
\v 5 So too the¦147475 tongue¦147476 is only small¦147477 but¦147480 can make extensive claims.
\p Yes, a tiny flame can ignite an entire forest¦147489
\v 6 and the tongue is a fire producing a torrent of depravity. It's a part of our physical body¦147513 but¦147491 capable of staining¦147510 us¦147507 all¦147511 over and setting¦147515 our worlds¦147496 on fire, because the tongue itself is set¦147522 on fire from hell.
\v 7 Every¦147526 kind of¦147529 wild¦147529 animal and birds¦147532 and reptiles¦147533 and sea¦147536 creature¦147536 can be tamed and has¦147540 been¦147540 tamed by¦147542 humankind¦147545
\v 8 but¦147548 no¦147550 one¦147550 is able to tame the¦147554 tongue¦147549—it's an¦147557 erratically evil¦147558 body part that's full¦147559 of¦147560 deadly¦147561 poison¦147560.
\v 9 We use it to bless¦147564 \add &our\add* \nd master¦147566\nd* and¦147568 father¦147569, yet we use it to curse¦147573 our fellow humans who are made in God's image.\x + \xo 3:9: \xt Gen 1:26.\x*
\v 10 Both blessings¦147587 and curses¦147589 come¦147586 out¦147586 of¦147582 the¦147583 same¦147584 mouth¦147585! My¦147593 brothers¦147592 and sisters¦147592, it shouldn't be¦147596 like that.
\v 11 Surely¦147597 a spring¦147601 flowing out¦147602 of a single cavity can't¦147597 produce both good-tasting and¦147609 bitter¦147611 \add water\add*.
\v 12 A fig¦147616 tree¦147616 can't produce olives¦147618 and a grape vine¦147621 can't produce figs¦147622, my¦147615 brothers¦147614 and sisters¦147614, just like a salty supply can't give fresh water¦147632.
\s1 Heavenly wisdom
